WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.234
Welcome to Building Defender XDR,

2
00:00:02.234 --> 00:00:06.212
a developer workbook in the
Federal Modernization Workbook.

3
00:00:06.212 --> 00:00:09.188
This is Chapter 8: the
monthly evidence run.

4
00:00:09.188 --> 00:00:13.286
The metrics measure what the controls
do, not whether a setting exists,

5
00:00:13.286 --> 00:00:17.526
and the evidence is a chain a security
officer can check with one command.

6
00:00:17.526 --> 00:00:22.053
We'll start from the guide's monthly run,
read the one command that replaces it,

7
00:00:22.053 --> 00:00:25.216
the metrics and the evidence
record, and walk two labs,

8
00:00:25.216 --> 00:00:29.600
including one where we change a recorded
file and watch the check catch it.

9
00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.468
The chapter has been
reviewed and approved;

10
00:00:32.468 --> 00:00:36.180
the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.218
Why one command, and why a chain?

12
00:00:42.218 --> 00:00:46.566
Because the guide's metrics measure what
the controls actually do, not whether

13
00:00:46.566 --> 00:00:50.580
a setting exists, and those numbers have
to come from the month's real runs.

14
00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.343
And because evidence the security officer

15
00:00:53.343 --> 00:00:56.030
can't verify alone is
just a folder of files.

16
00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:00.300
By the end of this chapter you can produce
the month's evidence package in one

17
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:02.536
command, read the guide's metrics from it,

18
00:01:02.536 --> 00:01:05.518
and show the security officer
it hasn't changed since.

19
00:01:05.518 --> 00:01:08.964
It's for the developers, the
platform owner who owns the run,

20
00:01:08.964 --> 00:01:11.760
and the security officer who receives it.

21
00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.310
Here's the guide's monthly run.

22
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:19.372
Connect with the two read scopes.

23
00:01:19.372 --> 00:01:21.975
Run three hunting queries, coverage,

24
00:01:21.975 --> 00:01:26.311
attack surface reduction and
prevention, into JSON files.

25
00:01:26.311 --> 00:01:30.283
Export the month's incidents,
filtered on created date time,

26
00:01:30.283 --> 00:01:32.472
which Learn lists as filterable.

27
00:01:32.472 --> 00:01:36.222
And hash the folder, so a
re-hash next month must match.

28
00:01:36.222 --> 00:01:40.697
The guide also gives seven metric
formulas, and an evidence catalog with

29
00:01:40.697 --> 00:01:45.380
each artifact's kind, gate and retention,
which are the program's design.

30
00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:50.467
What does that leave to you?

31
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:52.453
Seven gates' checks aren't in it,

32
00:01:52.453 --> 00:01:56.504
so the kit runs every check into
its own folder with a manifest.

33
00:01:56.504 --> 00:01:59.339
A hash list can be
regenerated after an edit,

34
00:01:59.339 --> 00:02:02.330
so each record holds the
hash of the one before.

35
00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:05.630
Files don't name their
controls, so each record does.

36
00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:10.612
A run that found five tickets hashes as
cleanly as one that found none, so each

37
00:02:10.612 --> 00:02:12.905
record carries the run's own result,

38
00:02:12.905 --> 00:02:16.068
and the go or no-go fails
until the findings close.

39
00:02:16.068 --> 00:02:19.805
Someone computes the metrics by
hand, so the kit writes them.

40
00:02:19.805 --> 00:02:23.247
And Graph carries no assignment
or containment time,

41
00:02:23.247 --> 00:02:28.070
so mean time to triage is reported as
not computed, with where to get it.

42
00:02:31.820 --> 00:02:37.037
One command runs every check from Chapters
1 to 7, each into its own folder,

43
00:02:37.037 --> 00:02:41.645
then the metrics, and with an
evidence path, records every folder.

44
00:02:41.645 --> 00:02:45.515
A check that throws, a missing
input or a refused session,

45
00:02:45.515 --> 00:02:48.207
is an error row, and the others still run.

46
00:02:48.207 --> 00:02:53.150
Each record holds the time, the environment,
the change record, the command,

47
00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:58.174
two results, the files' integrity and
the run's own check, the steps with their

48
00:02:58.174 --> 00:03:00.523
controls and indicators, the claim,

49
00:03:00.523 --> 00:03:03.683
every file's hash, and the
previous record's hash.

50
00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:06.858
The check re-hashes everything
and walks the chain.

51
00:03:06.858 --> 00:03:09.182
With a stage, it adds a row per step,

52
00:03:09.182 --> 00:03:13.490
which passes only with intact
files and a passing check.

53
00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:19.028
Here's where each metric comes from.

54
00:03:19.028 --> 00:03:23.064
Protected coverage and exception
exposure, from the coverage run.

55
00:03:23.064 --> 00:03:27.541
Enforcement depth, devices with
every applicable rule in block,

56
00:03:27.541 --> 00:03:30.665
from the A S R run against
the onboarded count.

57
00:03:30.665 --> 00:03:35.576
Prevention effectiveness, blocks per
thousand onboarded devices per week,

58
00:03:35.576 --> 00:03:39.552
from the prevention events and
the A S R events scaled to a week.

59
00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.216
Expired exclusions.

60
00:03:41.216 --> 00:03:43.952
Detection validity,
from the detection run.

61
00:03:43.952 --> 00:03:47.977
And synthetic-event success, from
the quarter's exercise record.

62
00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:50.925
Mean time to triage and to
contain isn't computed:

63
00:03:50.925 --> 00:03:56.010
the kit says to take it from the ticketing
system or the Action center history.

64
00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:00.810
Who's involved?

65
00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:05.060
The platform owner owns the run,
monthly, with the change record.

66
00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.665
The security officer receives
the metrics and the hash file,

67
00:04:08.665 --> 00:04:11.260
and re-checks last
month's folder this month.

68
00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:14.198
The security operations
lead runs the quarter's

69
00:04:14.198 --> 00:04:17.135
synthetic events and
files the exercise record.

70
00:04:17.135 --> 00:04:21.545
One caution about Lab 1: the
fixtures carry deliberate faults,

71
00:04:21.545 --> 00:04:25.610
so most runs record a failed
check, and the go or no-go fails.

72
00:04:25.610 --> 00:04:26.648
That's the point.

73
00:04:26.648 --> 00:04:30.750
Evidence of a finding is still
evidence, but it isn't a go.

74
00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.600
Lab 1, in the kit's folder.

75
00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:41.688
Step 1 runs the month and records
every run, with a lab change record.

76
00:04:41.688 --> 00:04:44.149
Ten run folders, ten records.

77
00:04:44.149 --> 00:04:47.228
Only signals and the metrics
pass their own checks;

78
00:04:47.228 --> 00:04:51.113
every other run found something,
as the earlier chapters showed.

79
00:04:51.113 --> 00:04:53.113
Step 2 reads the metrics.

80
00:04:53.113 --> 00:04:56.475
Protected coverage, sixty-four
point three percent.

81
00:04:56.475 --> 00:05:00.212
Two of eleven onboarded devices
have every rule in block.

82
00:05:00.212 --> 00:05:03.199
One of one enabled detection
has a passing test.

83
00:05:03.199 --> 00:05:05.912
Four of five synthetic
events did what they should.

84
00:05:05.912 --> 00:05:09.550
And mean time to triage
says not computed, and why.

85
00:05:13.300 --> 00:05:15.700
Step 3 reads the records' index.

86
00:05:15.700 --> 00:05:19.650
Result is the files' integrity,
and every record passes.

87
00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:22.838
Check result is what the
run found, and most fail.

88
00:05:22.838 --> 00:05:29.188
Step 4 checks the chain: every file matches
its hash, ten records, chain unbroken.

89
00:05:29.188 --> 00:05:34.772
Step 5 runs the production stage's go or
no-go, and it fails, saying why for each

90
00:05:34.772 --> 00:05:40.626
step: no pre-flight record, runs whose own
checks failed, and no scheduled run yet.

91
00:05:40.626 --> 00:05:43.225
Only signals and the metrics pass.

92
00:05:43.225 --> 00:05:47.920
The command exits with an error, which
is what a go or no-go should see.

93
00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:53.995
Step 6 is the one to remember.

94
00:05:53.995 --> 00:05:57.745
Append one line to a recorded
tickets file, and check again.

95
00:05:57.745 --> 00:05:58.908
Both checks fail.

96
00:05:58.908 --> 00:06:00.657
The sums say the file changed,

97
00:06:00.657 --> 00:06:04.595
and the chain says it no longer
matches its record, naming the file.

98
00:06:04.595 --> 00:06:07.820
That's what protects the evidence
from an edit after the fact.

99
00:06:07.820 --> 00:06:12.283
Step 7 runs the evidence
tests: all pass, none fail.

100
00:06:12.283 --> 00:06:14.692
Before step 6, you had a folder whose hash

101
00:06:14.692 --> 00:06:17.320
file you could hand to
the security officer.

102
00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:19.900
Delete the output folder when you're done.

103
00:06:23.650 --> 00:06:26.494
Lab 2 uses a test
tenant, never production,

104
00:06:26.494 --> 00:06:31.262
with every earlier chapter's Lab 2
inputs named in your settings file.

105
00:06:31.262 --> 00:06:33.187
Sign in with the read scopes.

106
00:06:33.187 --> 00:06:35.938
Run the month with an
evidence path on agency

107
00:06:35.938 --> 00:06:38.613
storage and the change
record, then check it.

108
00:06:38.613 --> 00:06:42.237
Give the metrics and the hash
file to the security officer.

109
00:06:42.237 --> 00:06:45.581
Next month, run the check
again on this month's folder:

110
00:06:45.581 --> 00:06:47.837
last month's files must still match.

111
00:06:47.837 --> 00:06:49.539
Nothing changed in the tenant,

112
00:06:49.539 --> 00:06:53.560
and the evidence stays with its
change record, so you just sign out.

113
00:06:57.310 --> 00:07:00.635
Here's what this gate proves,
against NIST's catalog.

114
00:07:00.635 --> 00:07:05.023
The month's metrics, from the month's
runs, support continuous monitoring.

115
00:07:05.023 --> 00:07:08.992
Each run recorded with its controls,
indicators, check result and

116
00:07:08.992 --> 00:07:13.623
hashes supports continuous monitoring
and protecting audit information.

117
00:07:13.623 --> 00:07:17.548
Kept and unchanged supports
audit protection and retention.

118
00:07:17.548 --> 00:07:21.748
And the exercise's synthetic events
support incident response testing.

119
00:07:21.748 --> 00:07:24.936
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
as the guide maps them.

120
00:07:24.936 --> 00:07:27.240
Each record carries the claim itself:

121
00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:31.020
it supports the controls, and
the assessor decides them.

122
00:07:34.770 --> 00:07:36.170
What breaks in production?

123
00:07:36.170 --> 00:07:41.337
A run with findings read as evidence of
a control leads to a go on a fleet with

124
00:07:41.337 --> 00:07:43.920
gaps; the check result in every record,

125
00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:47.670
and a go or no-go that fails
until the run passes, guard it.

126
00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:51.670
A file edited after recording is
caught by the hashes, by name.

127
00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:53.858
A removed record breaks the chain.

128
00:07:53.858 --> 00:07:59.132
Advanced hunting only keeps thirty days,
so the run files results every month.

129
00:07:59.132 --> 00:08:03.537
And a metric computed from a field that
doesn't hold it is a misleading number,

130
00:08:03.537 --> 00:08:07.040
so the kit says not computed,
and where the number lives.

131
00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:12.140
Here's what done looks like.

132
00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:15.602
The monthly run is scheduled
and runs with the change record.

133
00:08:15.602 --> 00:08:18.765
The metrics reach the
security officer each month.

134
00:08:18.765 --> 00:08:21.615
The check passes on last
month's folder this month.

135
00:08:21.615 --> 00:08:24.965
The quarter's exercise record
is named in the settings.

136
00:08:24.965 --> 00:08:27.675
And the evidence folder
is on agency storage,

137
00:08:27.675 --> 00:08:30.165
kept to the evidence catalog's retention.

138
00:08:30.165 --> 00:08:34.251
Next, Chapter 9 schedules this
run and takes the kit live,

139
00:08:34.251 --> 00:08:36.340
test first, then production.

140
00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:37.500
Thanks for watching.
